{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgApkSef1bR6mKmkw7knn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Soft 404s come from a `robots.txt` change, not from the pages themselves. I've fixed `robots.txt` and `site/app.js` and written up the cause and the fix in `ANSWER.md`. Nothing was deployed, so I couldn't check the fix against Google's live test; the only check run was `node --check` on `app.js`.\n\n**Cause:** On 2026-09-07, `Disallow: /v1/` was added to `robots.txt`. Each note page is an empty HTML shell, and `app.js` fills it in by fetching `/v1/notes/<slug>`. Googlebot obeys robots.txt for that fetch too, so it gets blocked. When any fetch fails, `app.js` shows \"NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address.\" Google therefore sees HTTP 200 with a \"not found\" page, which is the definition of a Soft 404. The URL inspection export shows exactly that: title \"Field notes\" and the not-found text. Browsers and curl ignore robots.txt, which is why the pages look fine to you. It also explains why the indexed count fell while you were adding notes: every page Google recrawled after the change became a Soft 404.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **`site/robots.txt`:** crawlers can now read the public endpoints (`/v1/notes`, `/v1/notes?…`, `/v1/notes/<slug>`). `/v1/notes/*/reactions`, `/v1/me`, `/v1/auth/token` and the rest of `/v1/` stay blocked, which was the point of the original rule. Google applies the longest matching rule, so the reactions block still wins.\n- **`site/app.js`:** \"NOT FOUND\" now appears only when the API actually returns 404, and in that case the page is also set to `noindex`. Any other failure shows \"Couldn't load this page\" instead of claiming the note doesn't exist.\n\nAfter you deploy, allow up to a day for Google to pick up the new `robots.txt`. Then re-run the live test on `/n/drip-line-pressure` and click \"Validate fix\" on the Soft 404 issue in Search Console. For the longer term, `ANSWER.md` suggests pre-rendering the note pages so indexing doesn't depend on Google running JavaScript."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_read_input_tokens":25133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5125156-b32d-47d6-9b90-7651cb1e4ca4","uuid":"3fe593fe-f968-41b2-b00d-e66aa969f635","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:41:31.484Z","request_id":"req_011CgApkSQGra3eRif7PE1wX"}
