ach note page is an empty HTML shell, and `app.js` fills it in by fetching `/v1/notes/<slug>`. Googlebot obeys robots.txt for that fetch too, so it gets blocked. When any fetch fails, `app.js` shows \"NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address.\" Google therefore sees HTTP 200 with a \"not found\" page, which is the definition of a Soft 404. The URL inspection export shows exactly that: title \"Field notes\" and the not-found text. Browsers and curl ignore robots.txt, which is why the pages look fine to you. It also explains why the indexed count fell while you were adding notes: every page Google recrawled after the change became a Soft 404.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **`site/robots.txt`:** crawlers can now read the public endpoints (`/v1/notes`, `/v1/notes?…`, `/v1/notes/<slug>`). `/v1/notes/*/reactions`, `/v1/me`, `/v1/auth/token` and the rest of `/v1/` stay blocked, which was the point of the original rule. Google applies the longest matching rule, so the reactions block still wins.\n- **`site/app.js`:** \"NOT FOUND\" now appears only when the API actually returns 404, and in that case the page is also set to `noindex`. Any other failure shows \"Couldn't load this page\" instead of claiming the note doesn't exist.\n\nAfter you deploy, allow up to a day for Google to pick up the new `robots.txt`. Then re-run the live test on `/n/drip-line-pressure` and click \"Validate fix\" on the Soft 404 issue in Search Console. For the longer term, `ANSWER.md` suggests pre-rendering the note pages so indexing doesn't depend on Google running JavaScript.","ttft_ms":2598,"type":"result","duration_ms":49631,"uuid":"85e0957c-1882-4849-87ce-642492dee429","ttft_stream_ms":1822,"time_to_request_ms":50,"first_content_frame_ms":1822,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
